  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a swashplate?",
      "a": "A swashplate is a pair of rings around the rotor mast, one fixed and one spinning with the blades. Sliding or tilting it changes the angle of each rotor blade, which is how a helicopter is steered.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a helicopter steer?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the cyclic stick do?",
      "a": "The cyclic tilts the swashplate, so each blade's angle changes once per turn. That tips the rotor disc, and the helicopter moves the way the disc tips: forward, backward or sideways.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a helicopter fly forward?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the collective lever do?",
      "a": "The collective raises or lowers the whole swashplate, changing all the blades' angles together. More angle means more lift for climbing; less means descending.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a helicopter climb?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are pitch links?",
      "a": "Pitch links are short rods joining the spinning ring of the swashplate to each blade. As the ring rises, falls or tilts, the links twist the blades to a new angle.",
      "asks": [
        "What connects the swashplate to the blades?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is it called cyclic?",
      "a": "Because each blade's angle changes in a cycle once every turn of the rotor: steeper on one part of the circle and flatter on the opposite part.",
      "asks": [
        "What does cyclic pitch mean?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Helicopter main rotors usually turn at a few hundred revolutions per minute.",
    "Because a spinning rotor behaves like a gyroscope, the blade angle change is timed roughly a quarter turn before the point where the disc should tip."
  ],
